    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 8,
      "bbox": [
        653,
        186,
        668,
        773
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "(a) ἀμελής has the same forms for the m. and f. (cf. εὔφρων, 82).",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 9,
      "bbox": [
        671,
        186,
        723,
        905
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "(b) γλυκύς types are of mixed declension 3-1-3 – the m. and n. declining like type 3 nouns, the f. declining like a 1st declension noun ἀπορία, but with short -α. (Cf. 90)",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 10,
      "bbox": [
        729,
        186,
        781,
        905
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "(c) The dominant vowel in the stem of both of these types of adjective is -ε-. Compare their declension with noun-types 3c, d, e and f (see 78, 79, 80, 127a).",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "order": 11,
      "bbox": [
        786,
        186,
        821,
        905
      ],
      "margin_number": null,
      "speaker": null,
      "text": "(d) υἱός ‘son’ can decline like 2a nouns, but it also decline like the m. form of γλυκύς (except for the acc. s.):",
      "lang": "mixed",
      "para_no": null,
      "marker": null,
      "table": null,
      "exercise": null,
      "caption": null,
      "uncertain": []
    }
